A||||5||William Federle||Battery Cables||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||04-04-2000||10:00 PM||||Does anyone know the proper routing of the positive battery cable for a 4 cyl. Fiero?<BR>My 1984 Fiero had the pos. cable routed too close to the exhaust and it finally broke in two about three miles from my home this afternoon after work. The haynes manual shows good pictures of the V6 routing but the 4 cyl pictures don't show much of the engine.<P>Thanks<P>If there's anyone out there with an 1984 Fiero SE with stock battery cable, I'd sure appreciate it if you would look at it and try to describe its routing to the starter.<BR>I'd like to make sure this doesn't happen again.<P>Thanks<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||04-05-2000||06:56 AM||||can anyone help?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||04-05-2000||12:44 PM||||I will look tonight and post tomorrow. (Unless someone else can answer quicker)<P>From my (poor) memory... It seems like there is some sort of plastic piece that mounts to the water pump area that it runs thru. Maybe a piece on the heat shield that holds the wire away from the exhaust???<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 04-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||04-06-2000||06:50 AM||||Thanks DRH. I replaced the cable last nite. I got a cable from Autozone along with some large-size ribbed wireloom. I routed it down along the waterpump like you said and tie-wrapped it to two holes in the waterpump housing to keep it away from the belt. Then I ran it under the AC compressor and up to the starter. I used the wire-loom along its<BR>entire length to try to give it some protection from heat. It isn't touching any normally hot components. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||04-06-2000||01:41 PM||||I looked on my 84 last night and that sounds right. On mine the cable runs thru a small space between the water pump and the timing gear cover. No AC. Also the lower part of the large heat shield between the exhaust and the block has about a 2-3" long by 1/2 wide metal "tab" sticking. This is wrapped around the cable to make sure it stays away from the exhaust pipe. My cable has the wire loom for it's entire length also.<P>As long as it can't touch moving parts and stays at least an inch or two from the exhaust you shouldn't have any problem.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||04-06-2000||06:57 PM||||Thanks again. I wondered what that metal tab was. I'm going to go right out and use it.<BR>||||reg||1
